NPF: the NetBSD packet filter, in its standalone form ("npfkern"), as used
by userspace network stacks.  On MINIX it runs inside the LWIP service.

Upstream:	https://github.com/rmind/npf
Imported:	commit 99f0756; dist/ is upstream's src/kern, src/libnpf and
		src/npfctl, with LICENSE and README.md.
License:	BSD-2-Clause (dist/LICENSE).
Depends on:	libnv (sys/external/bsd/libnv), thmap, libqsbr and liblpm
		(external/bsd/*), all built into minix/lib/libnpfkern.

Local changes (MINIX):
- dist/kern/stand/bpf.h: MINIX's <pcap/bpf.h> is NetBSD's <net/bpf.h>,
  which already has struct bpf_args and friends; use it (with
  __BPF_PRIVATE) instead of redefining them.
- dist/kern/stand/bpf_filter.c: <sys/endian.h> has be16dec()/be32dec().
- dist/kern/stand/npf_stand.h: ip_reass_packet() calls the host's
  npf_minix_ip_reass_packet() instead of failing with ENOTSUP.  The
  standalone stub made NPF drop every IPv4 fragment, because its
  "ip4.reassembly" parameter is on by default.  The LWIP service
  reassembles with lwIP's own code.

How it fits together:
- minix/lib/libnpfkern builds dist/kern (as upstream's src/kern/Makefile
  does, without npf_os.c) with the libraries above and a few libc pieces
  that system services lack (npf_minix_libc.c), as a private library.
  npf_minix_ctl.c runs control operations on packed nvlists.
- minix/net/lwip/pfil.c hooks NPF into lwIP: the IPv4/IPv6 input hooks,
  and output hooks that are MINIX-local to lwIP (liblwip patch 0005),
  called before fragmentation.  Packets looped back to a local address of
  another interface are filtered on that interface, not on lo0.
- minix/net/lwip/npfdev.c is /dev/npf (major 7, minor 128, cloning; it
  shares the BPF major).  Version and on/off switch are ioctls as on
  NetBSD.  Other operations are a write of the NetBSD ioctl number (as a
  uint64_t) followed by the packed nvlist request, and reads of the
  response: MINIX ioctls cannot carry nvlist_ref_t's pointers.

Behaviour:
- As on NetBSD, filtering is off until switched on ("npfctl start").  On,
  with no ruleset loaded, everything passes; a loaded ruleset blocks what
  it does not pass ("flush" loads pass-everything again).
- The LWIP service keeps NPF's state in memory: if the service restarts,
  filtering is off again and the ruleset is gone.
- "return-rst"/"return-icmp" rules block without sending a reply: the
  standalone NPF cannot send packets.
- A blocked outgoing packet fails the send with EHOSTUNREACH.
- Outgoing packets that are IPv4 fragments already (raw IP_HDRINCL sockets)
  are dropped while "ip4.reassembly" is on.
- NAT (PR 4) is not supported yet: NPF rewrites packets in place, and
  lwIP keeps TCP segments it may retransmit.

Tested by minix/tests/npf (testnpf.sh).

To update: copy upstream src/kern, src/libnpf and src/npfctl over dist/,
then re-apply the changes above.
